    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok, so if i do that, i'm basically just moving the data around to keep it clean. i'm worried that if i don't do this right, the database is just gonna get messy and cluttered with all these weirdly linked pieces of data that don't belong together. what's the main risk of leaving those transitive dependencies in there? like, what actually happens to the integrity of the data over time if i just ignore it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The main risk of leaving transitive dependencies is data redundancy and update anomalies. If you leave the department information repeated across many employee records, updating a department name requires changing it in every row where it appears, risking inconsistency if one update is missed. This leads to update anomalies, insertion anomalies (cannot add a department until you have an employee in it), and deletion anomalies (deleting the last employee in a department deletes the department information entirely).",
      "source": "target",
